12:42.120 --> 12:43.560
That is a high entropy region.

12:43.860 --> 12:44.120
Yeah.

12:44.120 --> 12:46.080
My path fundamentally changes.

12:46.480 --> 12:48.720
I'm swerving, braking, unpredictably.

12:49.080 --> 12:50.960
My trajectory is fragmenting.

12:51.280 --> 12:51.540
Yes.

12:51.660 --> 12:55.400
My path is no longer dictated just by the ideal geometry of the road.

12:55.560 --> 12:57.740
It is dictated by the disorder of the environment.

12:58.060 --> 12:59.960
That translates the mathematics perfectly.

13:00.360 --> 13:06.180
And what Flyxion proposes is that this tensor, Tiege, is the first hint of gravity.

13:06.380 --> 13:06.880
Wait, really?

13:07.120 --> 13:07.500
Gravity?

13:07.500 --> 13:07.980
Yes.

13:08.460 --> 13:13.380
What we currently perceive as base-time curvature, the bending of reality that causes gravity,

13:13.600 --> 13:19.280
is actually just the macro-level manifestation of these transport inconsistencies caused by entropy.

13:19.480 --> 13:19.920
Oh, wow.

13:20.180 --> 13:21.760
So gravity isn't a fundamental force.

13:21.860 --> 13:22.080
No.

13:22.320 --> 13:27.500
It is the physical experience of vector fields failing to align coherently in the presence of disorder.

13:27.680 --> 13:28.840
That is massive.

13:29.060 --> 13:30.440
But we need to zoom out a bit.

13:30.660 --> 13:35.240
Because everything we've discussed so far, the capacity, the transport, the entropy, the Lagrangian,

13:35.240 --> 13:36.620
these are all local rules.

13:36.820 --> 13:36.900
Right.

13:36.980 --> 13:41.100
They describe what happens at one specific point or one tiny patch of the mathematical manifold.

13:41.500 --> 13:43.540
But the universe is incredibly vast.

13:44.020 --> 13:48.820
How do these tiny local patches stitch themselves together to form the entire cosmos?

13:49.380 --> 13:54.800
This brings us to the core structural challenge of the paper, which we can think of as the local to global problem.

13:54.940 --> 13:55.140
Okay.

13:55.620 --> 14:01.020
Flyxion utilizes a branch of mathematics called sheaf theory to solve it.

14:01.220 --> 14:01.920
Sheaf theory.

14:02.040 --> 14:02.340
Yeah.

14:02.340 --> 14:08.520
In topology, you can't always describe a complex shape with one single coordinate system.

14:09.100 --> 14:10.620
Think of the surface of the Earth.

14:10.920 --> 14:11.140
Right.

14:11.220 --> 14:16.240
You can't draw a perfect flat map of the whole globe without distorting the continents.

14:16.420 --> 14:20.540
Like the Mercator projection makes Greenland look larger than Africa.

14:20.620 --> 14:21.780
Which is wildly inaccurate.

14:22.040 --> 14:22.340
Exactly.

14:22.680 --> 14:22.880
Right.

14:23.080 --> 14:25.520
To accurately map the Earth, you need an atlas,

14:25.920 --> 14:29.880
a collection of smaller local maps or patches that overlap each other.

14:30.000 --> 14:30.280
Okay.

14:30.280 --> 14:35.080
Sheaf theory is the mathematical language for managing data across these overlapping patches.

14:35.400 --> 14:40.160
So Flyxion proposes that the universe is an atlas of local RSVP configurations.

14:40.560 --> 14:40.920
Exactly.

14:41.480 --> 14:47.020
In each local patch, the scalar, vector, and entropy fields obey the Lagrangian equations perfectly.

14:47.200 --> 14:47.480
Okay.

14:47.620 --> 14:51.200
Flyxion defines this as the admissibility sheaf, or FRSVP.

14:51.460 --> 14:53.880
The primary problem of the universe is recursive gluing.

14:53.880 --> 15:04.440
Recursive gluing, meaning how do you ensure that the mathematical rules in patch A perfectly agree with the mathematical rules in patch B in the exact area where they overlap.

15:04.560 --> 15:05.120
You've got it.

15:05.380 --> 15:08.220
I think of this like taking a panoramic photo on a smartphone.

15:08.460 --> 15:10.000
Oh, that's a brilliant way to put it.

15:10.000 --> 15:16.600
You stand on a mountain, you pan your camera across the horizon, and the phone takes dozens of overlapping local pictures.

15:17.400 --> 15:22.560
The software's job is to stitch them together into one seamless global image.

15:22.780 --> 15:23.060
Right.

15:23.060 --> 15:29.180
But if your hand shakes, or if a bird flies through the frame while you are panning, the overlapping boundaries don't match.

15:29.260 --> 15:30.460
The software glitches.

15:30.540 --> 15:31.080
Yeah, exactly.

